<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vsevolod I of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1030">1030</a>–<a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Vsevolod Yaroslavich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vsevolod I, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, received Pereyaslavl on his father's death in 1054 and was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> from 1078 to 1093. He married a Byzantine princess, said to be of the Monomachos family, and was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a>. His reign in Kiev was troubled by quarrels with his nephews and by Cuman raids.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vsevolod-i-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Vsevolod I of Kiev" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radzivill_chronicle_245-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vsevolod Yaroslavich was the fifth and favourite son of Yaroslav I the Wise and Ingigerd Olafsdottir. A seal from his last years names him Andrei in Greek, which implies that his baptismal name was Andrew. To back an armistice with the Byzantine Emperor Constantine IX Monomachos in 1046, his father married him to a Byzantine princess, who according to tradition was called Anastasia or Maria. Their son bore the emperor's family name, Monomakh, which suggests she belonged to his close family, though no contemporary evidence attests the relationship. After her death in 1067 he married a Kypchak princess, Anna Polovetskaya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On his father's death in 1054 Vsevolod received the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslav</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and the township of Beloozero. With his elder brothers Iziaslav and Sviatoslav he formed a princely triumvirate that fought the steppe nomads and compiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a>, the first law code of the state. He made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, who first appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1055, but they invaded his principality in 1061 and routed him. In 1068 they defeated the three brothers on the Alta River, after which the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> rose in rebellion and proclaimed Vseslav their grand prince.</p><p>In 1073 Vsevolod and Sviatoslav forced Iziaslav to flee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. Sviatoslav died in 1076, and Iziaslav was killed in battle in a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oleg-sviatoslavich">Oleg Sviatoslavich</a>, who had allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Vsevolod took the Kievan throne as his father's only surviving son, uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and Pereyaslavl, and put his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> in charge of Chernigov. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> complains that the people no longer had access to the prince's justice and that judges became corrupt and venal. It also says he followed the advice of young councillors instead of his old retainers.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a>'s Autobiography he spoke five foreign languages. He lost most of his battles, and Vladimir did most of the fighting. In his last years Vsevolod was gravely ill and Vladimir presided over the government. His daughter Eupraxia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> married Emperor Henry IV.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vsevolod I of Kiev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Yaroslav the Wise died in 1054 he left it to his son <b>Vsevolod</b>, and it became a principality of its own, third in rank after Kiev and Chernigov.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-pereyaslavl">Principality of Pereyaslavl</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in Vyshhorod, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.45,30.52&amp;year=1070" aria-label="Kyiv on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5247 411 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vsevolod I of Kiev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.45,30.52&amp;year=1070">What was here in 1070? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
